{
  "info": {
    "name": "HustleHub+ API Test Suite (Part 2)",
    "description": "Covers authentication, gig management, bookings and transactions, income, role based access control and the Part 2 security controls.\n\nBefore a full run:\n\n1. Start the database and the API, and run \"npm run db:create-user\" once in the api folder if you have not already.\n2. Set AUTH_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=25 in api/.env and restart the API. The suite makes twenty-two requests to the authentication endpoints, and folder 10 then deliberately exhausts what is left.\n3. Restart the API between full runs. The rate limiter counts in memory, so a restart clears it without waiting out the fifteen minute window.\n4. Optional: to run the four admin checks, pass adminEmail and adminPassword (ADMIN_EMAIL and ADMIN_PASSWORD from api/.env) with newman --env-var or --global-var, or a Postman environment. Do not add them to this collection's variables: an empty collection variable hides a --global-var value and the admin checks would be skipped. Never save them in this file. Without them those requests are skipped.\n5. For a meaningful expired token check, run \"node generate-expired-token.js\" in the api folder and put the result in the expiredToken variable.\n\nWith Newman:\n\n  newman run postman/HustleHub_Part1_Tests_v3.postman_collection.json --insecure\n\n--insecure is required because the API uses a locally generated mkcert certificate.\n\nFolder 10 runs last on purpose. It exhausts the login rate limit, so any request to /api/login after it would be answered with 429.\n\nEach run creates three accounts with generated email addresses, one gig, and one booking with its transaction. The gig is deleted by the last request in folder 10. Accounts, bookings and transactions remain, because the API has no endpoints to delete them; clear the hustlehub database directly before recording a demo.",
    "schema": "https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json",
    "_postman_id": "d531f64d-0b4c-4025-83e4-3a6ab6941f4c"
  },
